A||||16||Ken Wittlief||The Fiero: A general assessment||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||04-21-2001||03:26 PM||||I've been looking into buying a Fiero for about a month now, and am driving 360 miles to see one on Sunday. This is the picture i have come up with, and i am interested in your comments if I got it right:<P>- the Fiero is the predecessor of the saturn, with its space frame and plastic body panels. Much of the sucess of the Saturn is due to the concepts worked out in the Fiero.<P>- the V6 / 5 speed version is a decent little sports car, with its mid engine design, hydralic cluch, and low center of gravity. The suspension and brakes are weak on the pre-88 versions, but can be made acceptable with aftermarket upgrades. the 88 holds its own with stock suspension, maybe lowering the springs a bit is a good idea.<P>- the 2.5L engine is pretty robust, but not something you will get much performance out of.<P>- the auto transmission is not popular with most fiero owners, esp the ones that want a 'sports car'<P>- the fire/recall problem in 84 gave the car a bad rep, and the fact that it has a limited appeal (momma cant go grocery shopping with it, ice cream melts in the 'trunk'...) so the general public has passed over the car and written it off.<P>   As a result you can now pick up a decent little sports car (V6/5speed) or a fun little commuter car (2.5/auto) for a very reasonable price, that has above average space frame protection (for a small car) and body panels that will never rust, great handling,  and lots of cool options that are easy to find like the sun roof, T tops, rear deck wing..., with a little care and attention the car should last well over 160k miles or more, and its not all that hard to work on and maintain or rebuild with average mechanical skills and an average set of tools and equipment.<P>  In other words, the Fiero as a 'cult car' may be one of americas best kept secrets!||||reg||1
Z||000001||grinthock||04-21-2001||03:32 PM||||You are SO right!<P>Although I don't agree that T-tops are easy to find!||||reg||8
Z||000002||Posthumane||04-21-2001||03:33 PM||||Sounds like an accurate assesment, although there is plenty you could do with a 2.5L s well. Good hunting.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||04-21-2001||04:21 PM||||Trunk never seems to get very hot, stuff can get warm in stop and go driving, but not kill the ice cream before you get home hot. Freeway driving keeps the trunk nice and cool.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||04-21-2001||04:24 PM||||Dont get me wrong, I love my Fiero, but in its day it was a good little sports car, but with advances in engine design in the past 15 years, the Fiero is a wee bit underpowered especially for its weight to compete with todays sports cars IMHO.  But then again I think the new MR2 is underpowered.<P>Now considering bang for the buck I think the Fiero is a great choice!  As far as autocrossing I am beating a good deal of corvettes made in the early to mid 80's, and some from the 90's while spending about 1/4 the money as the vette owners.<P>Go Ahead Flame Away!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72 Race Director<BR>#47 CSP<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">   <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mark||04-21-2001||04:33 PM||||Sounds like a pretty fair assessment, Ken.  <P>I find that some maintenance items, like heater core replacement, are much easier that other FWD cars while oil filter changes are somewhat of a pain in the a$$.<P>What I find most appealing is the uniqueness of this car.  Not too may plastic-bodied mid-engined two seaters to pick from!  <P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spektyr||04-21-2001||08:18 PM||||I just love confusing my friends with it...<P>"Where's the motor?"<P>"In the back."<P>"Oh, so the trunk's in front?"<P>"No, it's in the back too."<P>"So what's up front?"<P>"Not much..."<P>hehe<P>BTW Greg, how do the Fiero's do against the Miata's in Solo II Autocross?  My ex-fiance races a stock Miata.  I haven't been to an event since we split, but it'd be fun to show up and blow her doors clean off.  How much extra power am I going to need to pull that off?<P>If it moves, it can be made to move faster.<BR>If it can be made to move faster, it can be raced.<BR><P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||04-21-2001||08:30 PM||||Well the miatas are tricky...<BR>There are two types...<BR>I believe the 1.6 lire and the 1.8 litre<P>Comparing to the 1.6 litre its pretty even so it boils down to driver skill assuming you both have race rubber or similiar performance street tires, the 1.8's you had better be a better driver and have your suspension tuned for max performance within the rules and hope the 1.8 is bone stock.<P>Typically the women do not do as well as the men, but there are a few ladies out there who blow the doors off of most guys (including me) regardless of what they drive.  If your ex has been doing this for a while and has been competitive and you are a rookie, you are likely going to humiliate yourself.<P>To beat any miata you do not need extra power (this is not drag racing), you need to be a better driver...  that is why there are classes to compare similiar performance vehicles.||||reg||7
Z||000008||theogre||04-21-2001||08:49 PM||||Public didn't like it? sales numbers don't support that.<P>peek 4 cylinder performance depends what year. 87 4 cyl with Automatic tranny is allot stronger than many people would think. I get 0-65 allot faster than most people would think in mind. I pull onto highways at speed all the time. Don't forget.... the torque converter has a multiplier effect on engine tourque. The tourque entering the tranny is actually More than the motor is generating. So if the motor peaks at 135fp then the tranny sees over 150fp. (I think the TH125 TC multiplier is about 1.3.)<P>read in my cave for things about upgrading brakes..... <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A> I'll be adding some new observations to that article this weak.<P>Fire recall info is also in my cave. Applies to both V6 and L4 engines. Overall Fiero is no more a fire problem than any other car when it's leaking oil/cooant.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rex||04-21-2001||08:56 PM||||Experence has shown that every owner wants their car to run faster and handle better.<BR>What that leaves is looks. For the maker of the Fiero I dont believe they could have improved in one thing.Its the coolest little car on the road in looks and design. It is a pleasure to drive and own.This of course is based in vauue for the buck! ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tryxalon||04-21-2001||09:36 PM||||Actually, I would put the Fiero with a 2.5 liter with the manual (4 or Isuzu 5) sp as a "sports car" as in early Jags, MG-TD,MGA,MGB, Triumph Tr (whatever), Lotus Europa,  Fiat spyders, Pre 1958 Corvettes, Alpha Romeo Spyders, etcetera.  No, the engine is lower reving an torque-bound than those, but the feel of driving it is much the same: a car that makes you drive well to get the most out of it. <P>The V-6 is more of a "GT" or a "Muscle car with a good suspension" in the form of the 56 T-bird, the Early Datsun Z's, Porches, most Italian GT's, Motion Performance modified Camero's, Aston Martins, Corvettes after '61 or even the post 86 RX7. Sufficient power to make you grin, and an excellent platform that allows you to handle it safely. <P><BR>That is the main problem with the Fiero - being hard to "pidgeon-hole" into a "market segment" GM coundn't figure out what they did right ... but were afraid they were going to cut their own throats (IMHO). <P>This is what makes it a great car for all of us: You can leave it stock or personalize it in any form your imagination and wallet can fit a mid-engined, 2seat car into. <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 04-21-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000011||Tryxalon||04-21-2001||09:56 PM||||oops! Double post. <p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 04-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||fyerstarter||04-21-2001||10:30 PM||||My assessment of the Fiero is it's the funnest car I've ever owned, and it's also the most unreliable car I've ever owned. I swear it is broken down at least as much as it is running but then again its never soemthing too serious (yet) and even through all this I still want to get another Fiero! I don't understand it but they are addictive.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Will||04-21-2001||11:29 PM||||The trunk doesn't get hot (at least mine doesn't).<P>It's harder to work on than RWD cars, but no harder than most FWD cars.||||reg||1
Z||000014||mshill||04-21-2001||11:42 PM||||fyerstarter,  mine used to always have somrhting going wrong with it.  I finally did a total suspension, engine, and cooling system rebuild and I am going on 5K miles without a hitch (knock on wood).  The more I drive it the more I am confident in it's reliability, however, I will always carry a spare ignition module and tools to replace it in the trunk.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ed||04-22-2001||12:17 AM||||I like your response, tryxalon, about the "type" of car the GT is.  I've had both, the four and the GT.  The four handled better because it was lighter, but it didn't have the grunt for hills.  The GT takes a little more to get around the corners.  But, the GT is a "GT", and the four is an MG or Triumph type. And I have owned two English sports cars.  <P>I disagree a little about suspension and brakes, though.  While "everyone" wants better brakes and handling, even owners of high-end stuff like Porsches and others, I've got no complaints about my rear sway-bar equipped GT and its handling, nor about its brakes which I've worked hard on a racetrack.<P>P.S.  If you're a little unhappy about the handling of your six, use its power and the transmission to bring the rear around in corners.  That's a hoot!  But only on the aforementioned racetrack.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Spektyr||04-22-2001||01:31 PM||||Greg, my ex has the 1.8, but it's a rare auto tranny, so she doesn't have squat off the line, and it'll never downshift the way it should.  What she does have is better mid-range acceleration, competitive if not better handling, and much easier braking.  Not to mention the option of pulling the parking brake for hairpins.  (still not sure if I even want to try that in my Fiero given the location of the brake)<P>On skill... well I taught her how to race (not that I'm an expert or anything, I do ok), and she's never let it all hang out.  It's possible she's gotten brave enough to push that thing hard, but I doubt it.  And she won't spring for soft-compound tires, so I know I've got an edge there.<P>I got to play a bit with my Fiero today in an empty parking lot, but it wasn't big enough for me to really find out what happens if I overdo it in a corner.  I did learn that it handles a LOT different than a front engine car though.  More than I thought, I can't quite put my finger on what's different, but the few times I started to get a little sideways it just felt... different.  For one thing the front end seems like it's light enough to severely understeer, I'm hoping replacing the bushing and beefing up the anti-sway will help there.  New shocks and springs are in the car's near future as well.<P>So what suspension mods would bump me up a bracket in autocross?  What engine mods?<P>My final vision for the car is to stroke it to 3.1, turbocharge it, have polyurithane on all four corners, very nice shocks/struts, 1-1.5" drop coils, front and rear aftermarket anti-sways, and entirely new braking system (big master cylinder, calipers, vented rotors.)  I know that makes the stock Miata's no competition unless the driver's very good, but would I still be allowed to race them?<P>One final question.  I know the Miata's are pretty much un-rollable.  (never heard of one that was rubber-side up)  The Fiero seems like it'd skid long before it'd tip, is that true?  Has anyone rolled one?  (without hitting a pothole, curb, ditch, etc)<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
